.TH TRS 1 "March 2026" "trs 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trs \- safely move files to trash using the XDG Trash specification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.br
.B trs
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trs
(from TRaSh) is a secure CLI utility for moving files to trash instead
of permanently deleting them. It follows the XDG Trash specification,
making it compatible with desktop environments like GNOME and KDE.
.PP
Files are moved to:
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR or \fB~/.local/share/Trash/\fR (home trash)
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB$VOLUME/.Trash-$UID/\fR (volume trash for cross-device files)
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B trs [\fIFILE\fR...]
Move files or directories to trash.
.TP
.B trs list
List all files in trash with their index, name, size, and deletion date.
.TP
.B trs restore [\fINAME|INDEX\fR]
Restore files from trash. Without arguments, shows interactive selection
with fuzzy search. Use \fB--last\fR to restore the most recently trashed file.
.TP
.B trs empty [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Empty the trash. Use \fB--days N\fR to only remove files older than N days.
.TP
.B trs status
Show trash statistics (file count, size, locations).
.TP
.B trs version
Print version information.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Global Options"
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent files (when trashing).
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Remove directories and their contents recursively.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Verbose output.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable JSON output.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help message.
.SS "Restore Options"
.TP
.B \-\-last
Restore the most recently trashed file.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Overwrite existing files when restoring.
.SS "Empty Options"
.TP
.B \-\-days \fIN\fR
Only remove files older than N days.
.SS "Status Options"
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Show detailed statistics (oldest/newest dates, largest files).
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Custom data directory (default: \fB~/.local/share\fR).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, disable colored output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/Trash/files/
Directory containing trashed files.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/Trash/info/
Directory containing .trashinfo metadata files.
.TP
.B $VOLUME/.Trash-$UID/
Volume-specific trash directories for files on other filesystems.
.SH SECURITY
.B trs
implements multiple security measures:
.IP \[bu] 2
Symlink protection — never follows symlinks when copying or removing
.IP \[bu] 2
Path validation — prevents path traversal attacks
.IP \[bu] 2
System path protection — refuses to restore to critical system directories
.IP \[bu] 2
TrashInfo validation — validates paths from .trashinfo files
.IP \[bu] 2
DoS prevention — size and iteration limits on all operations
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B trs file.txt
Move a file to trash.
.TP
.B trs \-r directory/
Move a directory to trash (recursive).
.TP
.B trs \-f nonexistent
Force mode — ignore nonexistent files.
.TP
.B trs list \-\-json
List all trashed files in JSON format.
.TP
.B trs restore
Launch interactive TUI with fuzzy search.
.TP
.B trs restore \-\-last
Restore the most recently trashed file.
.TP
.B trs restore config.json
Restore a file by name.
.TP
.B trs restore 1
Restore a file by index (from \fBtrs list\fR).
.TP
.B trs empty \-\-days 7
Remove files older than 7 days.
.TP
.B trs status \-v
Show detailed trash statistics.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR gio (1),
.BR kioclient (1)
